    isbefore: []  # f619
    duplicate_of: []  # f619
    not_duplicate_of: []  # f619
    pr_ref:  # f619
    description: "INV-linub L3, residual shape 2 of 2. A CHAINED receiver --\n`File::create(p)?.write_all(...)` -- leaves no intermediate variable for the\n_var_types walker to type, so neither PR #595's written-path map nor the\nuse_aliases route can reach it.\n\nStrategy 1.9 already handles this shape for FIRST-PARTY targets: it calls\n_infer_type_from_rust_rhs on the receiver call_expression and resolves\n`Type::method` against the local/global symbol tables. As with the field-\nreceiver case, when the inferred type is external the lookup misses and the\ninferred type is dropped rather than carried into the module slot. The\ninference has already run; only the carry is missing.\n\nSEPARATE FROM the field-receiver shape (filed alongside) because the recovery\npoint is a different strategy and a different helper, and because\n_infer_type_from_rust_rhs returns a BARE name -- so this one additionally\nneeds the qualified-path treatment PR #595 introduced, applied to the RHS\ninference path rather than to let/param annotations. Do not assume one fix\ncovers both; ship a test for each shape.\n\nNOTE ON A DELIBERATE NARROWING IN #595: its shape-3 walker handles\n`let x = Type::assoc_fn()` but NOT `let x = Type::assoc_fn()?` -- the\ntry_expression wrapper. That was left alone on purpose so the walker mirrors\n_infer_type_from_rust_rhs exactly (which also does not unwrap `?`), rather\nthan having the two disagree about which node carries the type. Unwrapping\nbelongs in the shared helper, where both would get it, and that is this item.\nThe `?` form is pervasive in real rust I/O code.\n\nMEASURE THROUGH L4 ON FINDINGS, per INV-linub's standing rule.\nTripwire: 98/1206 catalogued trait-method sites matched post-#595."  # f619
    fields: {}  # f619
